<h2 class="flex whitespace-pre-wrap group font-semibold" id="targeting-specific-provider-endpoints"><div class="absolute" tabindex="-1"><a href="#targeting-specific-provider-endpoints" class="-ml-10 flex items-center opacity-0 border-0 group-hover:opacity-100 focus:opacity-100 focus:outline-0 group/link" aria-label="Navigate to header">​<div class="size-6 rounded-md flex items-center justify-center shadow-xs text-gray-400 dark:text-white/50 dark:bg-background-dark dark:brightness-[1.35] dark:ring-1 dark:hover:brightness-150 bg-white ring-1 ring-gray-400/30 dark:ring-gray-700/25 hover:ring-gray-400/60 dark:hover:ring-white/20 group-focus/link:border-2 group-focus/link:border-primary dark:group-focus/link:border-primary-light"><svg aria-hidden="true" width="18" height="18" stroke-width="2" class="size-3 shrink-0"><use href="#mi-mint-1-Link"></use></svg></div></a></div><span class="cursor-pointer">Targeting Specific Provider Endpoints</span></h2>
<span data-as="p">Each provider on OpenRouter may host multiple endpoints for the same model, such as a default endpoint and a specialized “turbo” endpoint, or region-specific endpoints like <code>google-vertex/us-east5</code>. To target a specific endpoint, you can use the copy button next to the provider name on the model detail page to obtain the exact provider slug.</span>
<h3 class="flex whitespace-pre-wrap group font-semibold" id="base-slug-matching"><div class="absolute" tabindex="-1"><a href="#base-slug-matching" class="-ml-10 flex items-center opacity-0 border-0 group-hover:opacity-100 focus:opacity-100 focus:outline-0 group/link" aria-label="Navigate to header">​<div class="size-6 rounded-md flex items-center justify-center shadow-xs text-gray-400 dark:text-white/50 dark:bg-background-dark dark:brightness-[1.35] dark:ring-1 dark:hover:brightness-150 bg-white ring-1 ring-gray-400/30 dark:ring-gray-700/25 hover:ring-gray-400/60 dark:hover:ring-white/20 group-focus/link:border-2 group-focus/link:border-primary dark:group-focus/link:border-primary-light"><svg aria-hidden="true" width="18" height="18" stroke-width="2" class="size-3 shrink-0"><use href="#mi-mint-1-Link"></use></svg></div></a></div><span class="cursor-pointer">Base Slug Matching</span></h3>
<span data-as="p">When you use a base provider slug (e.g. <code>&quot;google-vertex&quot;</code>) in any provider routing field (<code>order</code>, <code>only</code>, or <code>ignore</code>), it matches <strong>all</strong> eligible endpoints for that provider, including any variants or regions. For example, <code>&quot;google-vertex&quot;</code> matches <code>google-vertex</code>, <code>google-vertex/us-east5</code>, <code>google-vertex/us-central1</code>, and so on. A base slug does not by itself opt into a <a class="link" href="/docs/guides/features/service-tiers">service tier endpoint</a> (e.g. <code>openai/fast</code>, <code>google-vertex/flex</code>); select one with the <code>service_tier</code> parameter or its tier-suffixed slug. Tier endpoints already in the routing pool, such as priority endpoints of open-weight models, do match their base slug.</span>
<span data-as="p">To target a <strong>specific</strong> variant or region, use the full slug including the suffix (e.g. <code>&quot;google-vertex/us-east5&quot;</code> or <code>&quot;deepinfra/turbo&quot;</code>). When a provider serves the same variant from more than one region, the region follows the variant as its own segment (e.g. <code>&quot;baseten/fp4/us&quot;</code>); the variant slug alone (<code>&quot;baseten/fp4&quot;</code>) still matches every region of it.</span>
<span data-as="p">Every endpoint slug has the shape <code>provider[/variant][/region][/tier]</code>, with each segment present only when the endpoint needs it: the variant (a quantization such as <code>fp4</code>, or a provider-specific label such as <code>zdr</code>), then the region, then the <a class="link" href="/docs/guides/features/service-tiers">service tier</a> in the provider’s spelling (<code>priority</code>, <code>fast</code>, <code>turbo</code>, <code>flex</code>), always last. A tier endpoint’s slug is therefore its standard sibling’s slug plus the tier (<code>google-vertex/global</code> and <code>google-vertex/global/priority</code>, <code>baseten/fp4/us</code> and <code>baseten/fp4/us/priority</code>), and dropping a trailing segment matches every endpoint that shares the remaining prefix, except that dropping the tier never matches a tier endpoint.</span>
<!-- --><div role="presentation" style="position:relative;--scroll-area-corner-height:0px;--scroll-area-corner-width:0px" data-component-part="scroll-area" data-table-wrapper="true" class="min-w-0 [--page-padding:20px] flex w-[calc(100%+(var(--page-padding)*2))] my-[1em] py-[1em] -mx-(--page-padding) max-w-none [contain:inline-size]"><div data-id="base-ui-_R_a2aqqelubqoqe9qj4npfiutb_-viewport" tabindex="-1" class="size-full rounded-[inherit] [--scroll-area-fade-size:32px] overflow-y-hidden! base-ui-disable-scrollbar" style="overflow:scroll" data-component-part="scroll-area-viewport" role="region" aria-label="Scrollable table"><div role="presentation" style="min-width:fit-content" data-component-part="scroll-area-content" class="flex"><div class="px-(--page-padding) grow max-w-none table"><table class="m-0 min-w-full w-full max-w-none table [&amp;_th]:text-left [&amp;_td[data-numeric]]:tabular-nums [&amp;_td]:min-w-[150px]"><thead><tr><th>Slug in request</th><th>What it matches</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>&quot;google-vertex&quot;</code></td><td>All Google Vertex endpoints (every region)</td></tr><tr><td><code>&quot;google-vertex/us-east5&quot;</code></td><td>Only the <code>us-east5</code> region endpoint</td></tr><tr><td><code>&quot;deepinfra&quot;</code></td><td>All DeepInfra endpoints (default + turbo)</td></tr><tr><td><code>&quot;deepinfra/turbo&quot;</code></td><td>Only the DeepInfra turbo endpoint</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></div></div>
<h3 class="flex whitespace-pre-wrap group font-semibold" id="example-targeting-a-specific-endpoint-variant"><div class="absolute" tabindex="-1"><a href="#example-targeting-a-specific-endpoint-variant" class="-ml-10 flex items-center opacity-0 border-0 group-hover:opacity-100 focus:opacity-100 focus:outline-0 group/link" aria-label="Navigate to header">​<div class="size-6 rounded-md flex items-center justify-center shadow-xs text-gray-400 dark:text-white/50 dark:bg-background-dark dark:brightness-[1.35] dark:ring-1 dark:hover:brightness-150 bg-white ring-1 ring-gray-400/30 dark:ring-gray-700/25 hover:ring-gray-400/60 dark:hover:ring-white/20 group-focus/link:border-2 group-focus/link:border-primary dark:group-focus/link:border-primary-light"><svg aria-hidden="true" width="18" height="18" stroke-width="2" class="size-3 shrink-0"><use href="#mi-mint-1-Link"></use></svg></div></a></div><span class="cursor-pointer">Example: Targeting a specific endpoint variant</span></h3>
<span data-as="p">For example, DeepInfra offers DeepSeek R1 through multiple endpoints:</span>
<ul>
<li>Default endpoint with slug <code>deepinfra</code></li>
<li>Turbo endpoint with slug <code>deepinfra/turbo</code></li>
</ul>
<span data-as="p">By copying the exact provider slug and using it in your request’s <code>order</code> array, you can ensure your request is routed to the specific endpoint you want:</span>
