ark\":`#CE9178`},children:`        \"partition\": \"none\"`})}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.span,{className:`line`,children:_jsx(_components.span,{style:{color:`#0A3069`,\"--shiki-dark\":`#CE9178`},children:`      }`})}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.span,{className:`line`,children:_jsx(_components.span,{style:{color:`#0A3069`,\"--shiki-dark\":`#CE9178`},children:`    }`})}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.span,{className:`line`,children:_jsx(_components.span,{style:{color:`#0A3069`,\"--shiki-dark\":`#CE9178`},children:`  }'`})}),`\n`]})})})]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`In this example, if you have a BYOK key configured for OpenAI but not for Anthropic, OpenRouter can route to the GPT-4o endpoint using your own key even though Claude is listed first. Without `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`partition: \"none\"`}),`, the router would always try Claude’s endpoints first before falling back to GPT-4o.`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Note,{children:_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`BYOK endpoints are automatically prioritized when you have API keys configured for a provider and the request sets no `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`order`}),`. The `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`partition: \"none\"`}),` setting allows this prioritization to work across model boundaries. A request that sets `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`order`}),` is routed in that order, with your BYOK endpoint tried first within each provider you name; see `,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`/guides/overview/auth/byok#byok-with-provider-ordering`,children:`BYOK with Provider Ordering`}),`.`]})}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`2`,id:`ordering-specific-providers`,children:`Ordering Specific Providers`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`You can set the providers that OpenRouter will prioritize for your request using the `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`order`}),` field.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(Table,{children:[_jsx(_components.thead,{children:_jsxs(_components.tr,{children:[_jsx(_components.th,{children:`Field`}),_jsx(_components.th,{children:`Type`}),_jsx(_components.th,{children:`Default`}),_jsx(_components.th,{children:`Description`})]})}),_jsx(_components.tbody,{children:_jsxs(_components.tr,{children:[_jsx(_components.td,{children:_jsx(_components.code,{children:`order`})}),_jsx(_components.td,{children:`string[]`}),_jsx(_components.td,{\"data-numeric\":`true`,children:`-`}),_jsxs(_components.td,{children:[`List of provider slugs to try in order (e.g. `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`[\"anthropic\", \"openai\"]`}),`).`]})]})})]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`The router will prioritize providers in this list, and in this order, for the model you’re using. The order also outranks the priority your `,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`/guides/overview/auth/byok#byok-with-provider-ordering`,children:`BYOK keys`}),` would otherwise get: within each provider you name, your own key is tried before OpenRouter’s shared capacity. If you don’t set this field, the router will `,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`#price-based-load-balancing-default-strategy`,children:`load balance`}),` across the top providers to maximize uptime.`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Tip,{children:_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`You can use the copy button next to provider names on model pages to get the exact provider slug, including any variants like “/turbo”. See `,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`#targeting-specific-provider-endpoints`,children:`Targeting Specific Provider Endpoints`}),` for details.`]})}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`OpenRouter will try them one at a time and proceed to other providers if none are operational. If you don’t want to allow any other providers, you should `,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`#disabling-fallbacks`,children:`disable fallbacks`}),` as well.`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`3`,id:`example-specifying-providers-with-fallbacks`,children:`Example: Specifying providers with fallbacks`}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`This example skips over OpenAI (which doesn’t host Mixtral), tries Together, and then falls back to the norma